{# The colgroup and the header row of a sell-side document's line table, both built from the SAME
   ordered column list `sales_line_row.html.twig` renders its cells from.

   The three forms each carried their own copy of both, next to a third hand-written list of column
   names for the shared fee row, with a comment on each saying the three must not be allowed to
   drift apart. Three hand-maintained lists with a note asking someone to remember is not a
   mechanism — a header and a row that disagree about columns is exactly the defect a shared row
   invites, and it shows up as a table one cell out of alignment rather than as an error. Now there
   is one list per document and everything that has to count the columns counts the same thing.

   Column entries are {key, label, col, header}:

     key     what sales_line_row.html.twig renders in that position
     label   the column's name — the <th> text, and the row cells' data-label where the document
             labels them, and what the shared fee row spans by
     col     optional, the colgroup class SUFFIX for this column, so an order gets
             .order-col-qty and a quote .estimate-col-qty from one entry. Defaults to `key`.
     header  optional, the <th> text where it differs from `label`. Two documents need it: the
             quote's Actions column is labelled for the responsive CSS but deliberately shows an
             EMPTY header (the column is 26px of round buttons and a caption would not fit), and on
             the quote CREATE page the Price header reads "Price (optional)" while the cell's
             data-label stays "Price". Defaults to `label`.

   Parameters
   ----------
   columns  required  the column list, in order
   prefix   required for cols() only — 'order' or 'estimate', the colgroup class prefix. Both
                      tables are table-layout: fixed, so these <col> classes and not the cell
                      contents decide how the width divides. The standalone invoice's column set
                      IS order's own, so it calls cols() with prefix 'order' too, sizing off
                      .order-col-* rather than a table-specific copy of the same rules. #}

{% macro cols(columns, prefix) %}
    <colgroup>
        {%- for column in columns %}
        <col class="{{ prefix }}-col-{{ column.col|default(column.key) }}">
        {%- endfor %}
    </colgroup>
{% endmacro %}

{% macro head(columns) %}
    <tr>
        {%- for column in columns %}
        <th>{{ column.header is defined ? column.header : column.label|default('') }}</th>
        {%- endfor %}
    </tr>
{% endmacro %}
